1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,056
In this lesson, we'll learn what matrices
are and how to add and multiply them.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:11,555
A matrix is a rectangular array of
numbers. Its size is rows by columns.

3
00:00:12,005 --> 00:00:15,439
A two by three matrix has two rows and
three columns.

4
00:00:16,567 --> 00:00:23,367
Add matrices of the same size entry by
entry. Multiply by a scalar by multiplying

5
00:00:23,367 --> 00:00:24,375
every entry.

6
00:00:24,825 --> 00:00:29,262
Two times the matrix one, two, three, four
is two, four, six, eight.

7
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:35,925
To multiply A times B, each entry is a row
of A dotted with a column of B.

8
00:00:36,375 --> 00:00:41,758
One, two; three, four times five, six;
seven, eight gives nineteen, twenty two;

9
00:00:41,758 --> 00:00:43,053
forty three, fifty.

10
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:50,792
A times B needs A's columns to match B's
rows. And usually A B does not equal B A.

11
00:00:51,242 --> 00:00:57,537
The identity matrix, ones on the diagonal,
acts like the number one: A times I equals

12
00:00:57,537 --> 00:00:57,685
A.

13
00:00:58,833 --> 00:01:04,437
A store sells shirts at twenty dollars and
hats at fifteen. Monday it sold three

14
00:01:04,437 --> 00:01:08,220
shirts and two hats; Tuesday, one shirt
and four hats.

15
00:01:08,670 --> 00:01:13,768
The sales matrix times the price vector
gives ninety dollars Monday and eighty

16
00:01:13,768 --> 00:01:14,814
dollars Tuesday.

17
00:01:15,933 --> 00:01:19,816
Your turn. Multiply two, zero; one, three
times one; four.

18
00:01:20,266 --> 00:01:21,802
Pause the video and try it.

19
00:01:22,252 --> 00:01:28,524
Two times one plus zero times four is two.
One times one plus three times four is

20
00:01:28,524 --> 00:01:30,615
thirteen. So two; thirteen.

21
00:01:31,733 --> 00:01:36,725
In this lesson, we'll use inverse matrices
to solve systems of equations.

22
00:01:37,866 --> 00:01:43,200
The inverse of A, written A inverse,
satisfies A times A inverse equals the

23
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:43,840
identity.

24
00:01:44,290 --> 00:01:46,871
It plays the role of one over a for
numbers.

25
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:55,108
For a two by two matrix a, b; c, d, the
inverse is one over a d minus b c, times

26
00:01:55,108 --> 00:01:57,685
d, negative b; negative c, a.

27
00:01:58,135 --> 00:02:02,807
Swap the diagonal, negate the off
diagonal, divide by the determinant.

28
00:02:03,933 --> 00:02:10,452
The system two x plus y equals five, and
three x plus two y equals eight, becomes A

29
00:02:10,452 --> 00:02:12,808
times x, y equals five, eight.

30
00:02:13,258 --> 00:02:14,815
A is two, one; three, two.

31
00:02:15,933 --> 00:02:21,731
Here, a d minus b c is four minus three,
which is one. So A inverse is two,

32
00:02:21,731 --> 00:02:24,360
negative one; negative three, two.

33
00:02:24,810 --> 00:02:31,006
Multiply A inverse by five, eight: ten
minus eight is two, and negative fifteen

34
00:02:31,006 --> 00:02:35,242
plus sixteen is one. So x equals two and y
equals one.

35
00:02:36,366 --> 00:02:39,588
If a d minus b c is zero, there's no
inverse.

36
00:02:40,038 --> 00:02:43,280
Then the system has no solution or
infinitely many.

37
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:47,813
Your turn. Find the inverse of four,
seven; two, six.

38
00:02:48,263 --> 00:02:49,799
Pause the video and try it.

39
00:02:50,249 --> 00:02:55,773
The determinant is ten, so the inverse is
one tenth times six, negative seven;

40
00:02:55,773 --> 00:02:57,118
negative two, four.

41
00:02:58,266 --> 00:03:02,063
In this lesson, we'll compute determinants
and see what they tell us.

42
00:03:03,199 --> 00:03:06,869
The determinant of a, b; c, d is a d minus
b c.

43
00:03:07,319 --> 00:03:12,097
The determinant of three, five; one, two
is six minus five, which is one.

44
00:03:13,233 --> 00:03:18,481
For a three by three matrix, expand along
the top row: each entry times the

45
00:03:18,481 --> 00:03:22,470
determinant of what's left, with signs
plus, minus, plus.

46
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:25,459
These smaller determinants are called
minors.

47
00:03:26,599 --> 00:03:31,975
Find the determinant of one, two, three;
zero, four, five; one, zero, six.

48
00:03:32,425 --> 00:03:37,842
One times twenty four, minus two times
negative five, plus three times negative

49
00:03:37,842 --> 00:03:42,025
four: twenty four plus ten minus twelve,
which is twenty two.

50
00:03:43,166 --> 00:03:49,387
The determinant measures area. A triangle
with vertices zero, zero; four, one; and

51
00:03:49,387 --> 00:03:55,684
one, three has area one half the absolute
value of four times three minus one times

52
00:03:55,684 --> 00:03:55,987
one.

53
00:03:56,437 --> 00:03:57,696
That's eleven halves.

54
00:03:58,833 --> 00:04:04,633
Cramer's rule solves a system with
determinants. For two x plus y equals five

55
00:04:04,633 --> 00:04:07,345
and three x plus two y equals eight:

56
00:04:07,795 --> 00:04:13,157
x equals the determinant of five, one;
eight, two over one, which is two. y

57
00:04:13,157 --> 00:04:18,376
equals the determinant of two, five;
three, eight over one, which is one.

58
00:04:19,499 --> 00:04:23,553
Your turn. Find the determinant of six,
negative two; three, one.

59
00:04:24,003 --> 00:04:25,539
Pause the video and try it.

60
00:04:25,989 --> 00:04:27,951
Six plus six equals twelve.

61
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:34,419
In this lesson, we'll split a complicated
fraction into simple ones. It's a tool

62
00:04:34,419 --> 00:04:35,948
you'll use in calculus.

63
00:04:37,067 --> 00:04:44,043
Adding one over x minus one plus two over
x plus two gives three x over x minus one

64
00:04:44,043 --> 00:04:45,472
times x plus two.

65
00:04:45,922 --> 00:04:48,162
Partial fractions runs that backward.

66
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:56,951
Decompose five x minus one over x minus
one times x plus one. Write it as A over x

67
00:04:56,951 --> 00:05:00,031
minus one plus B over x plus one.

68
00:05:00,481 --> 00:05:07,014
Clear denominators: five x minus one
equals A times x plus one plus B times x

69
00:05:07,014 --> 00:05:07,862
minus one.

70
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,509
Plug in x equals one: four equals two A,
so A equals two. Plug in x equals negative

71
00:05:15,509 --> 00:05:20,136
one: negative six equals negative two B,
so B equals three.

72
00:05:20,586 --> 00:05:24,746
So it's two over x minus one plus three
over x plus one.

73
00:05:25,867 --> 00:05:31,808
For a repeated factor, include every
power. x plus three over x minus one

74
00:05:31,808 --> 00:05:36,853
squared is A over x minus one plus B over
x minus one squared.

75
00:05:37,303 --> 00:05:40,205
Matching gives A equals one and B equals
four.

76
00:05:41,333 --> 00:05:47,965
For an irreducible quadratic factor, use a
linear top. One over x times x squared

77
00:05:47,965 --> 00:05:52,960
plus one is A over x plus B x plus C over
x squared plus one.

78
00:05:53,410 --> 00:05:58,530
Solving gives A equals one, B equals
negative one, and C equals zero.

79
00:05:59,667 --> 00:06:03,549
Your turn. Decompose one over x times x
minus one.

80
00:06:03,999 --> 00:06:05,535
Pause the video and try it.

81
00:06:05,985 --> 00:06:09,676
Negative one over x plus one over x minus
one.

82
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:15,829
Next time, we start Unit 9, conic
sections. If this helped, subscribe to

83
00:06:15,829 --> 00:06:21,067
School Math, Explained, and turn on
notifications so you don't miss it. See

84
00:06:21,067 --> 00:06:21,765
you there.

